{"title":"2016 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 23","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1:_Super_WLOG\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1: Super WLOG</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2:_Conditional_Probability\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2: Conditional Probability</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3:_Calculus\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3: Calculus</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4:_Geometry\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4: Geometry</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5:_More_Calculus\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5: More Calculus</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_5.1_(better_explanation)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5.1 (better explanation)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_7:_More_WLOG,_Complementary_Probability\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7: More WLOG, Complementary Probability</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Solution_8:_3D_geometry\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 8: 3D geometry</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Solution_9(Fastest_solution_if_you_have_no_time):_Stick_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 9(Fastest solution if you have no time): Stick Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Punxsutawney_Phil\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Punxsutawney Phil</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_The_Art_of_Problem_Solving\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by The Art of Problem Solving</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Three numbers in the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/4/a14eeeecde5d3951c357b4a7e280daacdef23ae7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left[0,1\\right]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" /> are chosen independently and at random. What is the probability that the chosen numbers are the side lengths of a triangle with positive area?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/4/7642d19927814dd8de3eca57eb03b470f2a8d406.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }\\frac16\\qquad\\textbf{(B) }\\frac13\\qquad\\textbf{(C) }\\frac12\\qquad\\textbf{(D) }\\frac23\\qquad\\textbf{(E) }\\frac56$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"371\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC12_2006_A_23.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/42/AMC12_2006_A_23.png/500px-AMC12_2006_A_23.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"500\" height=\"385\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/42/AMC12_2006_A_23.png/750px-AMC12_2006_A_23.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/42/AMC12_2006_A_23.png/1000px-AMC12_2006_A_23.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1:_Super_WLOG\">Solution 1: Super WLOG</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1: Super WLOG\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Because we can let the the sides of the triangle be any variable we want, to make it easier for us when solving, let’s let the side lengths be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/2/692e3bee67c91837acbdcf63862b3b3c94366f28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"32\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. WLOG assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is the largest. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/0/43002e6e280cfd79b4c923ead9fd2b480e6ff0e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y&gt;a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"16\" />, meaning the solution is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/0/6a06610ecc504c327fe8b4fe0cee368426d4f556.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\;1/2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"72\" height=\"29\" />, as shown in the graph below.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/8/728a81800c2d06bbb7470db1e901910404bb8ff8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] pair A = (0,0); pair B = (1,0); pair C = (1,1); pair D = (0,1); pair E = (0,0);  draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); draw(B--D,dashed); fill(B--D--C--cycle,gray);  label(&quot;$0$&quot;,A,SW); label(&quot;$a$&quot;,B,S); label(&quot;$a$&quot;,D,W); label(&quot;$y$&quot;,(0,.5),W); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(.5,0),S); label(&quot;$x+y&gt;a$&quot;,(5/7,5/7)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"252\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2:_Conditional_Probability\">Solution 2: Conditional Probability</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2: Conditional Probability\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>WLOG, let the largest of the three numbers drawn be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/2/4c28242522f23e896074dc89612847f8317911d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />.  Then the other two numbers are drawn uniformly and independently from the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/b/68bacdc4b96052586744d16169ccd8c3233ed09f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,a]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />.  The probability that their sum is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/e/d9e79a047e35e7d5971ec3f0f30f49df52cfc4aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\;1/2.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"77\" height=\"29\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3:_Calculus\">Solution 3: Calculus</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3: Calculus\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/2/082852571ecc48ed136ba13dcffa8f6e40064bbd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&gt;b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />, consider two cases:\n</p><p>1) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/d/42d0153e2e0f64b23aa32adbc293122df5526299.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;a&lt;\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"78\" height=\"37\" />, then \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/9/6d987a28b05eb0878152b2b68ea0eaaaa4bbc6dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\int_{0}^{a}2b \\,\\text{d}b\\,\\text{d}a=\\frac{1}{24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"163\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p>2)<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/7/cc74e630a31bbbd3d21216dd2e5a9ca8a2eb3eb2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}&lt;a&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"79\" height=\"37\" />, then \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/6/8a6df757a1ae68af4730f34731c2c6e796dbdd5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{1} \\left(\\int_{0}^{1-a}2b \\,\\text{d}b + \\int_{1-a}^{a}1+b-a \\,db\\right)\\text{d}a=\\frac{5}{24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"355\" height=\"47\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/0/710e3e4f18d7c0270ab719a00adc61fa12363b1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" /> is the same. Thus the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4:_Geometry\">Solution 4: Geometry</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4: Geometry\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The probability of this occurring is the volume of the corresponding region within a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/8/c58deba0f41c4da22eb2c1016638fd874231c042.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\times 1 \\times 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"70\" height=\"12\" /> cube, where each point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c73ed093cc73a8d9a14185fa525e7067e5c3725d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y,z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> corresponds to a choice of values for each of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/5/0b56d32444d8efd65eb0cb6c01f8ac72c32587e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"32\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. The region where, WLOG, side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is too long, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/2/d72b305f5efbeaaf693f87af9ded5a0bd45aefcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z\\geq x+y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"15\" />, is a pyramid with a base of area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> and height <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so its volume is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/0/910b8d7425df17b8fadbd0318b52d3806c3b9ecc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1}{3}=\\frac{1}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" />. Accounting for the corresponding cases in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> multiplies our answer by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so we have excluded a total volume of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> from the space of possible probabilities. Subtracting this from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> leaves us with a final answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5:_More_Calculus\">Solution 5: More Calculus</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5: More Calculus\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The probability of this occurring is the volume of the corresponding region within a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/8/c58deba0f41c4da22eb2c1016638fd874231c042.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\times 1 \\times 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"70\" height=\"12\" /> cube, where each point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c73ed093cc73a8d9a14185fa525e7067e5c3725d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y,z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> corresponds to a choice of values for each of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/5/0b56d32444d8efd65eb0cb6c01f8ac72c32587e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"32\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />.  We take a horizontal cross section of the cube, essentially picking a value for z.  The area where the triangle inequality will not hold is when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/e/75e0e24e41ec135f6792371e26848a8b3f5d2ac3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + y &lt; z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"14\" />, which has area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c38220d8bae37fefd41c64fad605f4f1d56e467.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{z^2}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"19\" height=\"39\" /> or when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/d/17d80bbd71ff902c12773bccfd4e14b05d00871a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+z&lt;y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/3/8f368db2996d76ee0ee445bce95d0a8ae5bc7049.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y+z&lt;x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"14\" />, which have an area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/0/960ff99e1a7427d8a087d099c135f84dccc3f2f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(1-z)^2}{2}+\\frac{(1-z)^2}{2} = (1-z)^2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"244\" height=\"39\" /> Integrating this expression from 0 to 1 in the form\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/a/9aa60728b3b0f9daeb903b2bcbe8f72bf533b0d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_0^1 \\left(\\frac{z^2}{2} + (1-z)^2\\right) dz = \\bigg[\\frac{z^3}{2} - z^2 + z \\biggr |_0^1 = \\frac{1}{2} -1 + 1 = \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"455\" height=\"48\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_5.1_.28better_explanation.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5.1_(better_explanation)\">Solution 5.1 (better explanation)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5.1 (better explanation)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>This problem is going to require some geometric probability, so let's dive right in.\nTake three integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. Then for the triangle inequality to hold, the following <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> inequalities must be true\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/6/0e65db0b8389dfd472a55c1274043a286f85cf5f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x + y &gt; z\\]\" width=\"74\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/0/35088c1454f3a8eb3784803b9a172cfbaf4e270c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y + z &gt; x\\]\" width=\"75\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/f/54f986c96be6246471625118eb12e377d6abddfa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x + z &gt; y\\]\" width=\"74\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Now, it would be really easy if these equations only had two variables instead of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, because then we could graph it in a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-dimensional plane instead of a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-dimensional cube. So, we assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is a constant. We will deal with it later.\n</p><p>Now, since we are graphing, we should probably write these equations in terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> so they are in slope-intercept form and are easier to graph.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/0/0d0446b3ea98f137cf099161a9f5580ac4367f84.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y &gt; -x + z\\]\" width=\"88\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/4/ee41bff38c718c147d7dcf144a28a6684b925c8f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y &gt; x - z\\]\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/1/fd1419647f0441c5c0cac68574f47fa6872f3254.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y &lt; x + z\\]\" width=\"74\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Now, note that all solutions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> are in a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> square in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/7/3f79c3676b62fb47243e0d357c87115b6f3395e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"13\" />-plane because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/3/e63962294d4dc84c6c61245f6150e5f37b07dbe0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\in [0, 1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"70\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>I recommend drawing the following figure to get an idea of what is going on.\nThe first line is a line with a negative slope that cuts off a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/2/22231a5cdd4fe05a11f4b577e3b81221f3017a85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45-45-90$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"98\" height=\"13\" /> triangle with side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> of the bottom left corner of the square. The second line is a line with a positive slope that cuts off a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/2/22231a5cdd4fe05a11f4b577e3b81221f3017a85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45-45-90$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"98\" height=\"13\" /> triangle with side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/d/f4d27f40ba14a007cf8e6c2dd795da88704242f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> off the top left corner of the square. The third line also has a positive slope and cuts a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/2/22231a5cdd4fe05a11f4b577e3b81221f3017a85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45-45-90$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"98\" height=\"13\" /> triangle with side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/9/1897cefd5308cecbd8d00e100206cacf423c69f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 - z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> off the bottom right corner of the square. \n</p><p>Note: All triangles are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/2/22231a5cdd4fe05a11f4b577e3b81221f3017a85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45-45-90$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"98\" height=\"13\" /> because the lines have slopes of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae5aba07d37ff6ff813107e76260fb31ad5794e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Using the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/f/77f0cdbd0dbecd281387229d95a014017a0a395a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&gt;$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/2/262a080d0d87e973b817945967a512346d8bfc49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&lt;$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> signs in the lines, we see that the area that satisfies all three inequalities is the area not enclosed in the three triangles. So, our plan of attack will be to\n</p><p>Find the area of the triangles -&gt; Subtract that from the area of the square -&gt; Use probability to get the answer.\n</p><p>Except, now, we have one problem. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is still a variable. But, we want <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> to be a constant. Well, what if we just took the area over every possible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />? Well, that would be a bit hard, if not impossible to do by hand, but there is a handy math tool that will let us do that: the integral! \n</p><p>To find the area of the triangles, our plan of attack will be\n</p><p>Find the area in terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> -&gt; take the integral from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of the expression for the area (this will cover every possible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />\n</p><p>The area of the triangles is \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/b/92beba3474ffba993db0dcd10ffc1d69d7838813.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{z^2}{2} + (1-z)^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(-3x^2 + 4x\\right)\\]\" width=\"240\" height=\"39\" />.\n</p><p>The integral from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/b/ceb7db43c20ce353bcce9aaf5c5d6686d95ae68e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{2}\\int_0^1\\left(-3x^2 + 4x\\right)dx = \\frac{1}{2}\\]\" width=\"200\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p>The total area of all the possible unit squares is quite obviously\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/9/809f5df8c49e79d17ab991c21db889a6b3d8f6ae.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\int_0^11dx = 1\\]\" width=\"89\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p>Thus, the area not enclosed by the triangles is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1ce629f237c7647e58c011b4a2a78af2216597.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 - \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"84\" height=\"38\" />, and the total area of the square is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, the desired probability is \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/1/751783ac6019b34ae79abdcac105078275ab5d9c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{1} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac12}\\]\" width=\"96\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>~Extremelysupercooldude\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_7:_More_WLOG.2C_Complementary_Probability\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7:_More_WLOG,_Complementary_Probability\">Solution 7: More WLOG, Complementary Probability</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7: More WLOG, Complementary Probability\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The triangle inequality simplifies to considering only one case: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/8/198b22fe799225d451192790a96cfbda3e2ab150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{the smallest side+ the second smallest side} &gt; \\text{the largest side}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"446\" height=\"15\" />. Consider the complement (the same statement, except with a less than or equal to).  Assume (WLOG) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is the largest, so on average <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/f/aaf1f5f10f3c4e234a8e3407f3be0fa04ffa0bc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"20\" /> (now equal to becomes a degenerate case with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so we no longer need to consider it). We now want <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/b/ccbf66b6f5f9a8d29c16c85792263f8d2411b804.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b+c&lt;1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" />, so imagine choosing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/b/c9bbeb7caa1770d1d3cb842655f27e245b08188f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b+c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> at once rather than independently. But we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/b/c9bbeb7caa1770d1d3cb842655f27e245b08188f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b+c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> is between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. The complement is thus: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/1/bf1e99e7f8a903e5164889a972d067f0f906c7a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1/2-0)/2=1/4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"142\" height=\"18\" />. But keep in mind that we choose each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> randomly and independently, so if there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> ways to choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/b/c9bbeb7caa1770d1d3cb842655f27e245b08188f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b+c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> together, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/e/b8ea7ee8a282949fd853a01537ec7a2b55e56f75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2k$\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" /> ways to choose them separately, and therefore the complement actually doubles to match each case (a good example of this is to restrict b and c to integers such that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/e/a4ec832b2dc1dcb6a1cfb2da85ed4183256d77cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b+c=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"73\" height=\"16\" />, then we only count this once, but in reality: we have two cases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/3/3537ecc982707e8637f03c8470642bbcd57a2ab5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/2/a021347acd933e8bb33455fa2875bdc548bf3ad4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />; similar reasoning also generalizes to non-integral values). The complement is then actually <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/0/800ea59f6426d78dc6bdea43a3bae6a6436d9a56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2(1/4)=1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"18\" />. Therefore, our desired probability is given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/c/8ccd449463c978808074492e092c4669790891cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-\\text{complement}=1/2, C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"192\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_8:_3D_geometry\">Solution 8: 3D geometry</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 8: 3D geometry\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can draw a 3D space where each coordinate is in the range [0,1]. Drawing the lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/d/4ed2dff59d93aedb4f4edac4c07d3fab61198966.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y&gt;z, x+z&gt;y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"163\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/5/e45546d333038cecd5f61d2197a6041d24951657.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y+z&gt;x,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"80\" height=\"14\" /> We have a 3D space that consists of two tetrahedrons. One is a regular tetrahedron with side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/4/114e91200cd4c857d233c7fed6cbe7451f3a82fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" /> and the other has 3 sides of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/4/114e91200cd4c857d233c7fed6cbe7451f3a82fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" /> and 3 sides of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/d/6cdd1b53e5faf7a118222e4b7571d1e9afa0cf56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> The volume of this region is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/3/3e3a915d521036135df8f84dce16eac5ef6d2c98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 1 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />. Hence our solution is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/792daa030c96693198f143f9176bf888b7ca25cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C.$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_9.28Fastest_solution_if_you_have_no_time.29:_Stick_Solution\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_9(Fastest_solution_if_you_have_no_time):_Stick_Solution\">Solution 9(Fastest solution if you have no time): Stick Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 9(Fastest solution if you have no time): Stick Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider a stick of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Cutting the stick at two random points gives a triangle from the three new segments. These two random points must be on opposite sides of the halfway mark. Thus, after the first cut is made, there is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/9/7e9459aace7fb6d3519e7cf3151e8527fe2bfd46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac12}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"61\" height=\"49\" /> probability that the second cut is on the opposite side.\n</p><p>-this isn't necessarily true: what if the cuts are very very close to the edges? -happypi31415\n</p><p>-Think about it - Judokid\n</p><p>-alanisawesome2018\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Punxsutawney_Phil\">Video Solution by Punxsutawney Phil</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Punxsutawney Phil\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/DDRRP9VLYO8\">https://youtu.be/DDRRP9VLYO8</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_The_Art_of_Problem_Solving\">Video Solution by The Art of Problem Solving</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by The Art of Problem Solving\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FqRsTNB89ps&amp;list=PLyhPcpM8aMvI7N78mYZyatqveRU30iNcf&amp;index=3\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FqRsTNB89ps&amp;list=PLyhPcpM8aMvI7N78mYZyatqveRU30iNcf&amp;index=3</a> \n</p><p>- AMBRIGGS\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A\" title=\"2016 AMC 12A\">2016 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2016 AMC 12A Prob